    </table>
  </div>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Google recommends around 1500 x 1500 pixels or above for the main image. Its attribute table lists a ceiling of 64 megapixels and 16 MB. Method: direct reading of the published formatting specifications.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Google Merchant Center Help, Image link, undated, accessed October 23, 2026. https://support.google.com/merchants/answer/6324350?hl=en</p>

  <p>Keep exports below both ceilings. The Image too big help page says “less than” in its headline and “cannot be larger than” in its body. Avoid that boundary when exporting.</p>

  <p>Measure file weight and pixel count separately.</p>

  <p>Source: Google Merchant Center Help, Image too big, undated, accessed October 23, 2026. https://support.google.com/merchants/answer/12473903?hl=en</p>

  <h2>When does the new minimum image size apply?</h2>

  <p>Google’s attribute table already prints the incoming minimum. Its dedicated Image too small page explains the transition:</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">“Enforcement of the new requirements will begin January 31, 2027.” The new general minimum is 500 x 500 pixels. Until then, the stated floors remain 100 x 100 for non-clothing and 250 x 250 for clothing. Warnings began in July 2026. Method: read the dated transition notice alongside the temporary requirements on the same page.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: Google Merchant Center Help, Image too small, undated, accessed October 23, 2026. https://support.google.com/merchants/answer/12159030?hl=en</p>

  <p>The page gives an enforcement date but no dated announcement. For new main images, use the incoming minimum as your production floor. YouTube Shopping ads on TV already require 500 x 500 pixels. Lifestyle images have a higher attribute-specific minimum, below.</p>

  <p>A main image below the applicable minimum can cause product disapproval. Open Products, then Needs attention, and use the upcoming-enforcement warnings to find affected products. Check the file at the submitted URL: a small thumbnail can fail even when a larger master exists elsewhere. Open that URL before making another render.</p>

  <h2>What belongs in additional_image_link?</h2>

  <p>An additional image can show the reverse side. A close-up may explain a detail that disappears in the main view; a wider shot can show the item in use. They inherit the main image’s technical requirements, but Google permits staging and views that show only part of the product. An image of one component can also explain a bundle.</p>

